// Test 01: Lexical Structure - escape sequences (N2-09)
//
// THIS FIXTURE ASSERTS BYTES, NOT COMPILATION. The defect it pins was not a
// program that failed to build; it was a program that built and produced the
// wrong characters, silently, for as long as the lexer has existed:
//
//   "\\n"  denoted a backslash followed by `n`      and produced backslash + LINE FEED
//   "\0"   denoted a NUL                            and produced backslash + `0`
//
// `\0` is now REFUSED in a string literal (it stays legal in a char literal,
// where the value is just the integer 0): a String is a NUL-terminated
// `const char*`, so a NUL inside one is a value no String operation can see.
// See docs/specification/language-spec.md A2.
//
// The cause was a chain of `.replace()` calls: `"\\n"` was rewritten by the
// `\n` rule before the `\\` rule could claim the backslash. So every assertion
// below is on the RENDERED CHARACTERS, printed between markers, and the
// transcript is the contract.
//
// The escape set is `\n \t \r \" \\ \'` in strings (`\0` in chars only). Anything else is a compile error
// (tests/reject/unknown_escape.pd), not a pass-through.

fn main() {
    // The one that was wrong. `\\` is one backslash; the `n` after it is an
    // ordinary letter and must stay one.
    print("[\\n]");
    print("[\\t]");
    print("[\\\\]");

    // The five from grammar.ebnf, each on its own, between markers so a
    // swallowed or doubled character shows up in the diff.
    print("[\n]");
    print("[\t]");
    print("[\"]");
    print("[\\]");

    // `\r` is a carriage return, so the `]` overwrites the `[` on a terminal;
    // the transcript is compared as bytes, where both are present.
    print("[\r]");

    // A char literal takes the same escapes, and `'\''` is the only way to
    // write a quote. Printed as its code point, because that is what a char
    // literal denotes today (N4-04 is still owed).
    print_int('\n');
    print_int('\t');
    print_int('\r');
    print_int('\\');
    print_int('\'');
    print_int('\0');
    print_int('"');

    // An escape inside a longer string still resolves once, in place.
    print("a\tb\\tc");

    print("escapes done");
}
